StartUp.cs (like Global.asax)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

 public Startup(IConfiguration config){ // interface that helps reading config keys
    _config = config
 }
// to configure API service settings
//ConfigureServicce method will excute before Configure method (if defined).
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // Add entity framework SQL connection.
    services.AddDbContext<ApplicationDbContext>(options =>
        options.UseSqlServer(Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

    services.AddIdentity<ApplicationUser, IdentityRole>()
        .AddEntityFrameworkStores<ApplicationDbContext>()
        .AddDefaultTokenProviders();

    services.AddMvc();

    // Add application services.
    services.AddTransient<IEmailSender, AuthMessageSender>();
    services.AddTransient<ISmsSender, AuthMessageSender>();
}

public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IHostingEnvironment env, ILoggerFactory loggerFactory)
{
    loggerFactory.AddConsole(Configuration.GetSection("Logging"));
    loggerFactory.AddDebug();

    if (env.IsDevelopment())
    {
        app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
        app.UseDatabaseErrorPage();
        app.UseBrowserLink();
    }
    else
    {
        app.UseExceptionHandler("/Home/Error");
    }

    app.UseStaticFiles();

    app.UseIdentity();        
        
       //can chain multiple request delegates together with app.Use 
      app.Use(async (context, next) =>
        {
            // Do work that doesn't write to the Response.
            await next.Invoke();
            // Do logging or other work that doesn't write to the Response.
        });

    //MVC routing
    app.UseMvc(routes =>
    {
        routes.MapRoute(
            name: "default",
            template: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");
    });
    
    //each http response will be generated based on this anonymous method logic
    // app.Run delegate terminates the pipeline.
    app.Run(async context =>
        {
            //await context.Response.WriteAsync("Hello, World!");
            await context.Response.WriteAsync(_config("myKey"));
        });
}
Adding services to the services container makes them available within your application via dependency injection.

*CreateDefaultBuilder() :<inside Configure() in StartUp.cs> - it performs below jobs
    1. Setting up web server
    2. Loading configurations for the host & the application from various source (like launchsettingjson, appsetting.json)
    3. Configures logging service.        

-----------------------------Middleware------------------------------------------------------------
It consists of multiple delegates, that gets executed one by one. If any condition failed, it will not execute next pipeline delegate.
Response will be sent back once that delegate is executed. This mechanism is called short-circuit pipeline.

app.Run() method (in startUp-> configure method) :- each http response will be generated based on this anonymous method logic 
                                                    and terminates the pipeline.
 app.Use()- > can chain multiple request delegates together .
 
 
 Ordering of middleware delegate execution:-
1. Exception/error handling:-  1st exception will be executed :- app.UseExceptionHandler("/Home/Error");
2. Static file server :- 2nd static file request:- app.UseStaticFiles();     
3. Authentication:- 3rd authentication :-  app.UseIdentity();      
4. MVC:-  4th routing :- app.UseMvcWithDefaultRoute(); or app.Mvc()

* Middleware helps in processing request and response based on our need. For example one middleware component takes care of request authentication , another for exception handeling.
    1. it has access to both request and response object.
    2. it may simply pass request to next middleware
    3. it may process & pass the request to next middlewware. 
    4. may handle the request and short circuit the pipeline (return the response)
    5. may process the out going response. 
    6. middleware are executed in the order they are added to the pipeline .

************************  Introduction ***************
.net core sdk is installed on development machine and is required to build and run an app.
.net core runtime is required on staging/ production machine and is reuired to run deployment of .net packaged project.

* .csproj can be edited directly without unloading the project, in .net code.
* All files that are defined under project are by default part of the project. Hence .csproj does not hold any reference of files and folders.
* .net core application starts as console application.

********************  AspNetCoreHostingModel *********************************
1. InProcess hosting ->
    a. InProcess hosts the app inside the IIS worker process. ( process name w3wp.exe / iisexpress.exe)
    b. InProcess hosting is faster than OutOfProcess hosting. Because conectivity between 2 servers in OutOfProcess hosting hits performance.
    c. Process name IIS express/ IIS.
2. OutOfProcess hosting ->
    a. this kind of hosting model forwards web request to Kestrel server and the .net core project will be hostested on that server.
    b. Two web servers are involved. Internal web server - Kestrel & External web server-> IIS/ Nginx /Apache.  
    c. Process name is dotnet.exe

*Kestrel Server:- It's a cross platform webserver for ASP.Net core. It can be used as an edge/ standalone server where request can be 
                  handled by this server itself or can be used as a backend server. Kestrel uses dotnet.exe process to host .net app.

* command :- dotnet run (on project folder path) : will build and run the .net core app.
* When .net core project is run using command prompt, internally Kestrel server hosts the application. 
* Default hosting model for .net core project is OutOfProcess hosting.
* A reverse proxy server can be configured between browser and Kestrel server (i.e IIS/NginX / Apache) so that an extra layer of security and load balancer can be configured.
* If a .net core project is run via command prompt, by default it uses OutOfProcess hosting model even when AspNetCoreHostingModel is defined as "InProcess" in launchsettings.json.
* If command name is "IISExpress" & AspNetCoreHostingModel is "InProcess", then only .net core will use InProcess hosting model. Rest all cases it will use out of process hosting model.

****************************** settings files *********************************
1. launchsettings.json - Resides under properties in project node (only in dev environemnt)
                         Application URL is set here. 
                         Environmental variables are set here.
                         if command name is "project", .net will use OutOfProcess hosting model.
2. appsetings.json :- similar to web.config. We can define application/ environment variable/ keys here.                         
3. appsettings.development.json - dev environment specific settings. Hence keys defined in appsettings.json will be overridden .
4. appsettings.production.json

* Priorities of a variable defined under different files W.R.T. IConfiguration service (a service that is being used to read config keys) in descending order.
    1. command line argument
    2. Environmental variable (defined in system environment)
    3. User secret
    4. appsettings.development.json / appsettings.production.json
    5. appsettings.json    
    
*WebHost.CreateDefaultBuilder() :- is the method where all these config keys are read by the app.
    
    
    
    
    






